{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33333c5f9e17ebfba5f07ca7d626998c5fedc99a56aea32bfae09d25d64721a4"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "stable"] ["text" "unstable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "stable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "stable"] ["text" "unstable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "unstable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a saddle point"] ["text" "a stable node (sink)"] ["text" "an unstable node (source)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-331517" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a saddle point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33333c5f9e17ebfba5f07ca7d626998c5fedc99a56aea32bfae09d25d64721a4"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/83a565accc834d7c16091de22eb43ba80c0ce9c4290f567d1e0ccc009beca565.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33333c5f9e17ebfba5f07ca7d626998c5fedc99a56aea32bfae09d25d64721a4"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-332710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33333c5f9e17ebfba5f07ca7d626998c5fedc99a56aea32bfae09d25d64721a4"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0c70e2c185d054b8cc62411736ecec2e60a716d265819a82a171076d999ef4e6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/85cb5c01c72b8b903cc0b5fc8c593c0b6e96550380e6bc7af55d5db5b5f40d4e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/b414b76f1fe20b5238e75281e57bb851c8e67777e225d7610631dab1b4921b82.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/d19a6d30c12f5d966578cd950db1f16a9bc309f3a24a8e2480e52edca1281f75.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/19cd34ffc84e4a904d87cdfe0d8b6721861a0a39025484946dbe471a5ea4ad87.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33333c5f9e17ebfba5f07ca7d626998c5fedc99a56aea32bfae09d25d64721a4"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "stable"] ["text" "unstable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "unstable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757f69569bbee58"} {"question" "q-333216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "stable"] ["text" "unstable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/state.json" "file_sha256" "be23bc16b54fa573350a6c781ce4708887c9780aea1393d27757